## ABSTRACT
The metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are expected as ideal biomimetic enzymes for colorimetric glucose detection because of their large surface areas, well defined pore structures, tunable chemical composition, and multi-functional sites. However, the intrinsically chemical instability and low mimetic enzyme activity of MOFs hinder the application of them in imitating the enzyme reactions. In this work, we demonstrated a metal-MOF synergistic catalysis strategy, by loading Pt nanoparticles (Pt NPs) on MIL-88B- $\cdot N H_{2}$ (Fe-MOF) to increase peroxidase-like activity for the detection of glucose. The induced electrons transfer from Pt atom to Fe atom accelerated the redox cycling of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}/\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , improved the overall efficiency of the peroxidase-like reaction, and enabled the efficient and robust colorimetric glucose detection, which was proved by both experiments and density functional theory (DFT) calculation. Additionally, the sensitivity and chemical stability of this synergistic effect strategy to detect the glucose are not affected by the complex external factors, which represented a great potential in fast, easy, sensitive, and specific recognition of clinical diabetes.
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic frameworks, Pt nanoparticles, synergistic effect, glucose detection
## Introduction
Glucose plays an important role in biology as the energy source of living cells and the intermediate product of metabolism, which is the main energy supply substance of living organisms. Nevertheless, when there is excessive glucose in the blood, it can cause a range of diseases, such as diabetes [1], which can lead to death in severe cases [2, 3]. Therefore, rapid and sensitive detection of glucose is significantly important in-clinic diagnosis. Various analytical techniques, like colorimetry [4–6], fluorescence [7, 8], surface-enhanced Raman scattering [9, 10], chemiluminescence [11, 12], and electrochemistry [13, 14] have been developed to detect glucose. Among them, the enzyme colorimetric assay which can fast, sensitively, and specifically identify glucose through their distinct color change to the target analytes, has been widely used for glucose detection [15]. However, it may fail to display an impressive performance in body fluids containing complex substrate components because the activity of the enzyme is easily reduced or even denatured affected by the heavy metal ions and small molecules, like natural lectin and antibodies. To achieve the goal of stable and robust measurement of glucose, many nanomaterials with enzyme-like characteristics (named nanozymes) have been developed to mimic peroxidase, such as CuS [12], Pt nanoclusters [16], $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ [17], and N-doped carbon [18].
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of coordination polymers formed by the connection of transition metal ions or clusters as nodes and polydentate organic ligands as linker, are featured with periodic porous network structure [19–21]. With large surface areas, well-defined pore structures, tunable chemical composition, and readily accessible active sites [19, 22, 23], MOFs are considered as potential artificial nanozymes to imitate the catalytic reaction activities of natural peroxidase enzyme. In fact, over the past decades, considerable research efforts have been mostly focused on designing and synthesizing new MOF structures and investigating their peroxidase-like catalytic application. Liu et al. developed a photosensitive metal– organic framework (PSMOF) with light-responsive oxidase-like activity to detect glutathione in normal and cancer cells [24]. Zhou’s group systematically introduced the mechanism of stable MOF, design and synthesis of stable MOF structure, and its latest applications [25]. Nevertheless, the intrinsically chemical instability and low activity of MOFs, which generated from the inorganic nodes in most of the MOF structures are generally blocked by the organic linkers, limiting their full potential in peroxidase-like reactions [26, 27]. Because of the advantage of MOFs in effectively combining with various functional nanomaterials through their great surface area and adsorption capabilities, the strategy of MOF-supported metal nanoparticles (MNPs) were promoted for improving the peroxidase-like performance [22, 28, 29].
In the present work, we demonstrate a synergistic catalysis strategy by loading Pt NPs on Fe-MOF (Pt/Fe-MOF) to improve the peroxidase mimics for efficient colorimetric detection of glucose (Scheme 1). Herein, Pt NPs connect $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group on the Fe-MOF linker with coordinate bonds, thus forming stable MOF-supported Pt NPs and generating synergistic interactions between the Pt NPs and the Fe-MOF matrix. The induced electrons transfer from the Pt atom to the Fe atom, accelerates the redox cycling of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and dramatically improves the overall efficiency of the peroxidase-like reaction. The corresponding $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ can catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the product of glucose oxidation by glucose oxidase, to produce •OH, which oxidizes the colorless $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) to blue oxidation state TMB (Ox-TMB). This activated colorimetric change of TMB was readily employed for glucose detection in serum samples. It is hopefully to be used in the clinical diagnosis of diabetes. Our study details also provide a deeper understanding and insight into the synergistic effect of MOF-supported MNPs composites, which thus in turn will direct the engineering of MOF-based functional nanomaterials.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals and materials
Pluronic F127 (EO97PO69EO97, with an average $\mathrm{Mn}=12,600\mathrm{\Omega}$ ), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC), lactose, maltose, sucrose, sodium hydrogen phosphate dihydrate, hexane, and sodium dihydrogen phosphate anhydrous were purchased from Sun Chemical Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd. Terephthalic (TA), $\mathrm{H_{2}P t C l_{6}{\bullet}6H_{2}O}$ , polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP, $\mathrm{Mw}=58{,}000\mathrm{\Omega}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl_{3}}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , CH3COOH, CH3COONa, glucose oxidase (GOx), glucose, mannitol, L-galactose, acetone, ethylene glycol, 1,2- diaminobenzene (OPD), hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $30\%)$ , and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azino-bis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid) (ABTS)
were all purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. TMB was obtained from Shanghai Adamas Reagent Co., Ltd. Ultrapure water $(\ge18~\mathrm{M}\Omega$ , Millipore) was used in all experiments.
## 2.2  Synthesis of Pt/Fe-MOF
$1.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of as-synthesized Pt NPs solution $\mathrm{{\langle1\mg/mL)}}$ was added to $12~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water of Fe-MOF $\mathrm{~(2~mg/mL)}$ stirring with the speed of $750\mathrm{rpm/min}$ . Then, under room temperature, the mixed solution was further stirred for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ . Subsequently, Pt/Fe-MOF was collected by centrifugation at $10,000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 5 min and washed three times with water. Finally, the obtained Pt/Fe-MOF was dried overnight at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for later use.
## 2.3  Peroxidase catalytic activity of Pt/Fe-MOF
Typically, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Pt NPs $(2.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ , Fe-MOF $\mathrm{(1~mg/mL)}$ , and Pt/Fe-MOF $\mathrm{(1~mg/mL)}$ was added into sodium acetate $(\mathrm{Na}{-}\mathrm{Ac})$ buffer solution (0.1 M, $\mathrm{pH}=4.0$ ) containing TMB $(0.25~\mathrm{mM})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\langle0.25~mM\rangle}$ , respectively. The absorbance at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was measured with a ultraviolet (UV)–visible (vis) absorption spectrometer. Material $+\ \mathrm{Na}{-}\mathrm{Ac}$ buffer solution was used as a blank to eliminate its own interference.
## 2.4  Detection of glucose
The detection of glucose was performed under the following conditions. First, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of glucose oxidase $(2~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ and $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of glucose solution of different concentrations were incubated at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then the solution consisted of $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ $\mathrm{(1~mg/mL)}$ ), $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the TMB $\mathrm{{\dot{1}0}~m M)}$ , and $1.6~\mathrm{mL}$ of the $_{\mathrm{{Na-Ac}}}$ buffer solution was added. The mixture was incubated for another $10~\mathrm{min}$ and was used for the absorption spectrum measurement.
In order to detect glucose concentration in human serum, the serum samples were pretreated. First, the serum samples stored at minus $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were melted at room temperature. The serum samples were centrifuged at $8{,}000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ and collected the supernatant. Finally, the glucose concentration in serum was determined according to the above method.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of Pt/Fe-MOF
Accordingly, the two absorption bands of 3,463.0 and $3,345.0\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from the FT-IR spectrum of $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ were disappeared, which indicated that all the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ groups in Fe-MOF were covalently bonded with Pt NPs.
The lattice spacing of the Pt NPs anchoring on the Fe-MOF is $0.23\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was consistent with the Pt (111) (the insert figure in Fig. 1(b)). Meanwhile, the energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) images (Fig. 1(c), Fig. S6 in the ESM) showed strong C, Fe, N, O, and Pt signals in all the samples suggesting the successful anchoring of Pt NPs over the whole sample surface, it further proved that Pt NPs were successfully loaded on Fe-MOF. As indicated by the inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) measurement, the loaded Pt was $3.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Table S1 in the ESM). Besides, the XRD pattern of $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ confirmed that the loaded platinum particles did not affect the crystal structure of Fe-MOF. It may be due to the low concentration of Pt NPs on the Fe-MOF surface and the small size of Pt NPs, the corresponding characteristic peaks of Pt nanocrystals did not appear in the XRD spectrum. The chemical composition analysis of prepared samples by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) revealed that five main constituent elements were existent in hybrid nanozymes. As depicted in Fig. S7 in the ESM, the corresponding signals of five main elements C1s, N1s, O1s, Fe2p, and Pt4f were located at 284.8, 399.6, 531.7, 711.5, and $71.2\mathrm{eV}$ in XPS spectra. This also illustrated the successful combination of Pt NPs and Fe-MOF. According to all the above characterization results, Pt/Fe-MOF was successfully prepared.
## 3.2  Enhanced peroxidase-like activity of Pt/Fe-MOF hybrid nanozymes
Because of the synergetic effect between Pt NPs and Fe-MOF, the Pt/Fe-MOF exhibited excellent peroxidase-like activity. This peroxidase-like activity of Pt/Fe-MOF was firstly investigated by catalyzing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ oxidizing the typical chromomeric substrate TMB, ABTS, and OPD, respectively. As shown in Fig. 2(a), the Pt/Fe-MOF could readily oxidize the colorless TMB, ABTS, and OPD solutions into blue, green, and yellow, respectively with the characteristic absorption peaks appearing at the corresponding positions (652, 420, and $452\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) in the UV–vis spectra. Furthermore, the color-changing of TMB was monitored to evaluate the catalytic performance of Pt/Fe-MOF.
As shown in Fig. 2(b), both Fe-MOF and Pt/Fe-MOF could readily oxidize the colorless TMB into blue. The results confirmed that both Fe-MOF and Pt/Fe-MOF had peroxidase-like activity, but the Pt/Fe-MOF presents a more superior catalytic performance than Fe-MOF. For quantitative comparison, if the UV–vis absorbance at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in the Pt/Fe-MOF system is set as a reference $(100\%)$ , the one in the Fe-MOF system is calculated $42.8\%$ and $7.0\%$ in the $\mathrm{Pt}$ system. Obviously, compared with simple Fe-MOF and Pt NPs, the catalytic ability of Pt/Fe-MOF is improved by 1.0 time, under the same conditions (Fig. 2(c)). Considering both the Barrett–Joyner– Halenda (BJH) pore size distributions and Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) surface area for Fe-MOF and $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ have little change after merely loaded $3.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Pt NPs (Fig. S8 in the ESM), the dramatically improved catalytic performance shows a synergistic effect between the two components of Pt and Fe-MOF after the combination. Meantime, compared to the reaction velocity of Pt NPs and Fe-MOF (Fig. 2(d)), the higher velocity of Pt/Fe-MOF system has been evidently exhibited.
In general, the enzyme-like activity was easily affected by pH and temperature [32–34]. The investigation for peroxidase-like activity of Pt/Fe-MOF at different pH values and temperatures has been proceeded (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). The highest activity of Pt/Fe-MOF could be observed at acid conditions $\mathrm{(pH=4.0)}$ . And the optimal temperature of Pt/Fe-MOF hybrid nanozymes was $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Moreover, the peroxidase-like activity of Pt/Fe-MOF also increased with the feeding concentration (Fig. 3(c)). Furthermore, Pt/Fe-MOF maintained the original activity despite undergoing seven repetitions, indicating that it had excellent reusability and peroxidase-like activity (Fig. 3(d)). Therefore, $\mathrm{pH}=4.0$ and $T=35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were selected as the optimal conditions for glucose detection.
The enzyme kinetics of Pt/Fe-MOF was also studied through the Michaelis–Menten curve of Pt/Fe-MOF (Figs. S9(a)–S9(d) in the ESM) by measuring the absorbance changes of products with different concentrations of TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [35, 36]. The maximum reaction rates (Vmax) and Michaelis–Menten constants $(\mathrm{Km})$ values were calculated from the fitted Lineweaver-Burk plots. As shown in Table S2 in the ESM, the Km of Pt/Fe-MOF was much smaller than that of the nanozymes that have been reported [37, 38], indicating that it has a higher affinity with TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The value of Vmax was also higher than those of other nanozymes, indicating that Pt/Fe-MOF had a superior catalytic performance.
## 3.3  Mechanism of Pt/Fe-MOF as peroxidase mimetics
The peroxidase-like activity of iron-based compounds has been reported by many studies [39, 40]. The typical examples were $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and ${\tt a}{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ NPs [41, 42]. At the same time, other studies had reported detection of •OH in reactions, it was mainly produced by releasing free $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ [43]. And the conventional peroxidase-like reaction, involving the redox cycling of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ proceeds through the following equations:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{complex})}\\ &{\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{complex})\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+{\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{H}}+\mathrm{H}^{+}}\\ &{\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+{\bullet\mathrm{OH}}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}}\end{array}
$$
In the above process, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ forms a surface complex with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ on the surface of Pt/Fe-MOF. Then, electron transfer occurs between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in the complex, generating $\mathrm{\cdot{O}_{2}H}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in the reduced state. Finally, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ reacts with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate •OH. Compared with the reactions between $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the reduction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is the rate-limiting step due to the low rate constant $(9.1\times10^{-7}\mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ [52]. Therefore, in most of these Fe-based peroxidase catalysts, the overall catalytic performance is often depressed [44–46].
In order to prove the above, the enhanced synergetic peroxidase-like activity of Pt/Fe-MOF was systemically studied by XPS. The color change of TMB was mainly due to the fact that Pt/Fe-MOF could catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ , the product of the reaction between glucose and glucose oxidase $\operatorname{\Pi}(\operatorname{GOx})$ , to produce •OH. Therefore, we studied the XPS of Fe and $\mathrm{Pt}$ in the presence and absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 4(a), the characteristic peaks of Fe 2p appeared at positions 725.4 and $711.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ which were generated by the spin of Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ orbitals respectively. The deconvolution results of the spectra showed that Fe existed in the form of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . However, compared with absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the peak position after the reaction shifted to lower binding energy, indicating that the valence state of Fe in Pt/Fe-MOF has changed. The split peak results shown that Fe mainly existed the form of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ with the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, as depicted in Fig. 4(b), the valence state of Pt NPs changed from $\mathrm{Pt}$ to $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ . The peak separation results showed that the peak position shifted to the high binding energy direction and part of the electrons were transferred to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , accelerating the redox cycling of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and improving the overall efficiency of the peroxidaselike reaction [47, 48].
The electron spin resonance (ESR) spectroscopy in Fig. 5(a) was used to verify the peroxidase-like activity of Pt/Fe-MOF by the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce •OH. As shown in Fig. 5(a), compared with the control group, after adding trace amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in Pt/Fe-MOF, the characteristic 1:2:2:1 signal peak of 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) captured •OH was observed in ESR spectrum, but no signal was detected in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , indicating that the hybrid nanozymes could catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce •OH. At the same time, to determine the formation of •OH, TA was selected as a fluorescent probe to track •OH and generate TA-OH with characteristic peak at $450\mathrm{nm}$ [49, 50]. As shown in Fig. 5(b), the fluorescence intensity of $\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF+\DeltaTA+H_{2}O_{2}}$ was significantly enhanced at $450\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating that •OH was produced during the reaction. However, Pt/Fe-MOF and $\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF+TA}$ also have weak peaks at $450~\mathrm{nm}$ due to the characteristic peak of Fe-MOF at $450~\mathrm{nm}$ . The above results shown that Pt/Fe-MOF mainly catalyze the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce •OH, which oxidizes TMB to $_\mathrm{Ox}$ -TMB and achieving the colorimetric detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose.
## 3.4  Detection of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and glucose
With the efficient and robust synergetic peroxidase-like catalysis, the Pt/Fe-MOF can be used for colorimetric detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose. The curve of absorbance and concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was gained under the optimal conditions. As illustrated in Fig. S10 in the ESM, the absorbance at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ enhanced with the increasing concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and a calibration linearity curve of Absorbance $=0.00104$ $\mathrm{\small{[H_{2}O_{2}]}}+0.07252$ was calculated with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration ranging from 20 to $600\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The detection limit of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was as low as $13.01\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Simultaneously, the selectivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was tested using $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , etc. as interferences. As demonstrated in Fig. S11 in the ESM, only $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ had an obvious response, in other words, the interference of other ions can be ignored.
To verify the applications of $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ in colorimetric detection of glucose, the experiment was carefully studied, under the optimal conditions obtained from the above experiments. Figure 6(a) and Fig. S12(a) in the ESM revealed that the signal response at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ increased gradually with the increment of glucose concentration. The relationship between ultraviolet absorbance at the wavelength of $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and different glucose concentrations was shown in Fig. 6(b) and Fig. S12(b) in the ESM. The fitted results showed that Pt/Fe-MOF had a good linear relationship $R^{2}=0.993)$ when used to detect different concentrations of glucose, which developed the way for the quantitative detection of glucose. To test the selectivity of this method, lactose, maltose, sucrose, mannitol, and L-galactose were selected as interferences. As shown in Fig. 6(c), the effects of these interfering substances could be negligible compared with glucose, indicating that the Pt/Fe-MOF hybrid nanozymes had a good selectivity, due to the high catalytic affinity of glucose oxidase’s towards glucose. At the same time, considering the storage problem of Pt/Fe-MOF hybrid nanozymes, the stability of the composite was also tested. As shown in Fig. 6(d), even Pt/Fe-MOF solutions stored for 50 days still maintained $90\%$ enzyme-like activity when used to detect glucose, which verified that Pt/Fe-MOF hybrid nanozyme behaves excellent enzyme stability.
Based on the above results, the detection limit of the $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ prepared in this article was $2.3~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , which reaches the normal requirement of detecting glucose in serum samples $(3.9{-}6.4\ \mathrm{mM})$ ) [51]. Compared with the other reports on detecting glucose (Table S5 in the ESM), this peroxidase-like colorimetric detection method possesses two main advantages. One is a short response time for glucose detection where the color change from colorless to blue was just within $2~\mathrm{min}$ , the other is the detection limit is lower than most others. It illustrates that the $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ has enhanced peroxides mimic activity due to the synergistic effect among Pt NPs and Fe-MOF.
## 3.5  Detection of glucose in the serum samples
To further investigate whether the Pt/Fe-MOF could be used to detect glucose in human serum samples, human serum samples were diluted to different concentrations in the linear range of the standard curve and then added to the proposed GOx-Pt/Fe-MOF-TMB system for testing. The serum glucose concentration in healthy and diabetic patients was calculated from the standard curve. As shown in Table 1, compared with the standard value detected by the hospital standard glucose oxidase method (GOD-POD) biochemical analyzer, the glucose concentration detected by the GOx-Pt/Fe-MOF-TMB system was closed to the true value within the experimental error range. These results showed that our proposed colorimetric detection method had excellent accuracy and reliability, and could be applied to the early diagnosis of diabetes.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have developed an innovative strategy for ultrasensitive and robust detection of glucose by loading Pt NPs on the Fe-MOF. Benefitted from the “synergetic effect” between Pt atom and Fe-MOF, the electrons were transferred from $\mathrm{Pt}$ atom to Fe atom, overcoming the rate-limiting step of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ conversion and improving the overall efficiency of the peroxidase-like reaction. Combined with the Pt NPs, the $_{\mathrm{Pt/Fe-MOF}}$ brings 1.0 time enhanced catalytic rate and is able to maintain high catalytic activity. The significant mimic peroxidase activity of Pt/Fe-MOF hybrid nanozymes has been readily used to colorimetric detection of glucose in human serum samples, thus showing promising potential for clinical detection of glucose. Our study details also provide a deeper understanding and insight into the synergistic effect of MOFsupported MNPs composites, which in turn will direct the design and engineer of MOF-based functional nanomaterials.
